        <fig id="article-35600.image.f1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Focal Arteriolar Narrowing. Narrowing of a retinal arteriole (marked with &#x0003c;) indicates hypertensive retinopathy changes. Contributed by Koushik Tripathy, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="focal-narrowing" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-35600.s17">
        <fig id="article-35600.image.f2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Arteriovenous Crossing Changes. This illustration shows the Salus sign, the Gunn sign, and the Bonnet sign, which are characteristic vascular changes in hypertensive retinopathy. Contributed by&#x000a0;Koushik Tripathy, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="htnr__av" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-35600.s18">
        <fig id="article-35600.image.f3" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Malignant Hypertensive Retinopathy. The image shows optic disc edema (papilledema), cotton wool spots, arteriovenous crossing changes, and internal limiting membrane striae in a patient with malignant hypertension. The other eye displayed similar features. Contributed by Koushik Tripathy, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="htnr" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-35600.s19">
        <fig id="article-35600.image.f4" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Exudative Retinal Detachment and Elschnig Spots in Hypertensive Choroidopathy. This ultrawide field image reveals an inferior exudative retinal detachment, visible between eyelash artifacts at the lower part of the image. The superotemporal and superonasal retina display signs of hypertensive choroidopathy, including multiple Elschnig spots. Similar findings were present in the patient&#x02019;s other eye. Contributed by Koushik Tripathy, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="exudative-rd" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
